  "source": {
    "title": "Portadown Express, 16 January 1914, p. 5 (\"Urban Council Elections: Portadown's New Council\"); Lurgan Mail, 17 January 1914, p. 4 (\"Portadown\")",
    "publisher": "Portadown Express (via the British Newspaper Archive)",
    "kind": "news",
    "check": "every figure read from the Portadown Express page image; the Lurgan Mail image agrees except Retalic (524)"
  },
  "note": "Triennial election of the whole council (fifteen seats, one area), Thursday 15 January 1914; William Wilson, town clerk, was returning officer and declared the poll from the Town Hall balcony at 10.20 p.m. F. W. White and W. G. Hewitt had resigned before their terms ended, so the outgoing council had 13 members. \"The number of voting papers issued was 1641, and of these only 2 were spoiled\"; 575 of the voters on the register were women (the register total is damaged on the page). The Lurgan Mail marks the old members (a): C. Johnston, Bell, W. J. Johnston, W. H. Wright, Locke, Thornton, R. Wright, Sloan, Fulton, Anderson and Gregory (defeated); it omits Gough and Palmer, prints Retalic 524 (Portadown Express 523), and marks James Grew \"(N.)\". The Portadown Express prints \"Gegory\". No party labels printed. Charles Johnston: no party label printed in 1914. At other elections he stood as Unionist (Portadown, Armagh County Council, 31 May 1911, printed 'Unionist': Belfast News-Letter, 2 June 1911, p. 11 (\"Armagh County Council\"); candidates in the Newry Reporter, 6 May 1911, p. 5 (\"Armagh County Council\": withdrawals, contests and unopposed returns)). Robert Anderson: no party label printed in 1914. At other elections he stood as Unionist (South-East, 15 January 1920, printed 'U.': Lurgan Mail, 24 January 1920, p. 5 (\"Portadown Election\")). His labels elsewhere differ (UUP x5, Independent Unionist x1)."
}
